\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Study domains and observation distribution for California's Sierra Nevada and Lake Tahoe (left) and Colorado's Rocky Mountains (right). Within each block: (A) study extent (red) within the state; (B) MRoS reports over shaded mountainous relief, colored by reported phase, with meteorological stations as grey triangles; (C) kernel density of report elevation by phase; and (D) monthly report counts by phase. Panel C is density-normalized within each phase, so the curves compare shape rather than volume, and phase totals appear in the legend. California's reports are bimodal in elevation, rain-dominated near 1500~m and snow-dominated near 1950~m, whereas Colorado's peak near 1700~m. Note how MRoS reports tend to cluster along road corridors and near population centers, with occasional far-off reports in more remote parts of the mountains.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
