\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Data-assimilation and machine-learning architecture. (a) Input data: crowdsourced MRoS phase reports, surface station networks (HADS, LCD, WCC, MADIS), GPM IMERG probability of liquid precipitation (pLP), and the USGS 3DEP elevation model resampled from 1m to 1km resolution. (b) Assimilation and interpolation: layers of resampled elevation and IMERG pLP are assimilated with kriged station observations and MRoS reports to produce hourly, 1km gridded predictor surfaces. (c) ML Architecture: an XGBoost snow-versus-rain classifier is fit separately per domain on the assimilated, interpolated predictors, scores are beta-calibrated, and a Guassian wet-bulb-conditioned band of Equation~\ref {eq:band} is applied. (d) Output: snow, rain, and an uncertainty flag that represents where the model abstained from making a confident call.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:architecture}{{2}{5}{}{figure.2}{}}
