\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces What the crowdsourced surfaces contribute at the phase boundary: near-freezing precision and recall against wet-bulb temperature for the full configuration (blue) and the same configuration with the MRoS surfaces removed (red), for California (top) and Colorado (bottom), snow (left) and rain (right). Held-out test split, scored at the raw $0.5$ threshold with no uncertainty band applied. Observer-reported mix is excluded and shading marks $|T_w| \le \ensuremath  {2\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}$. Colorado's curves cross repeatedly rather than separating. Its individual bins rest on tens of observations, so those crossings sit well inside sampling noise and the interval-based ablation of Table~\ref {tab:ablation} remains the appropriate test there. Figure~\ref {fig:mros-contrib-tair} repeats the comparison against air temperature.}}{12}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mros-contrib}{{4}{12}{}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Held-out accuracy by air temperature bin for MRoS-XGB (black) and the best benchmark in each domain (blue), with 95\% cluster-bootstrap confidence bands, for California (left) and Colorado (right). The shaded \ensuremath  {0\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}--\ensuremath  {4\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}} region marks the range of 50\% rain--snow air-temperature thresholds reported across sites by \citet  {jennings2018spatial}, shown for reference. The model's advantage grows across this range as threshold methods lose skill. Accuracy is evaluated on rain and snow observations in \ensuremath  {1\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}} bins plotted at midpoints, with bins holding fewer than 20 observations dropped. Ribbons are 2.5th--97.5th percentiles of 500 cluster-bootstrap resamples over \mbox  {30-km} $\times $ calendar-day blocks. ``Best benchmark'' is unique per domain.}}{12}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tair}{{5}{12}{}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Held-out test performance of the model and the nine benchmark partitioning methods, with 95\% cluster-bootstrap confidence intervals (5{,}000 resamples; 30-km $\times $ calendar-day clusters). Methods are ordered by overall accuracy within each domain. NF = near-freezing subset ($|T_w| \le \ensuremath  {2\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}$; $n = 782$ in CA, $n = 179$ in CO). Point estimates are computed once on the full test set and are not medians over resamples, so intervals need not be symmetric about them. Per-phase recall and bias for the model are given in Table~\ref {tab:bootstrap}, and the paired model-minus-benchmark differences underlying the significance claims of Section~\ref {sec:bench-results} appear in Figure~\ref {fig:forest}.}}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:benchmark}{{5}{13}{}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Paired model-minus-benchmark accuracy differences with 95\% cluster-bootstrap confidence intervals: overall (top) and near-freezing (bottom) for California (left) and Colorado (right). Every California interval lies entirely above zero. In Colorado all overall intervals do too, and only the near-freezing difference against $T_d = \ensuremath  {0.5\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}$ reaches back across it. Differences are paired within each resample, so each interval reflects the variability of the \emph  {difference} on common data rather than of two independently resampled quantities. Intervals are 2.5th--97.5th percentiles of 5{,}000 resamples over \mbox  {30-km} $\times $ calendar-day clusters. They quantify test-set sampling uncertainty only, propagating neither uncertainty in the kriged surfaces nor retraining variability, either of which would widen them.}}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:forest}{{6}{13}{}{figure.6}{}}
