\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Critical success index in \ensuremath  {2\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}} air-temperature bins for the model and the nine benchmarks, California (top) and Colorado (bottom), scored once with snow as the event class (left) and once with rain (right). Held-out test split, with bins holding fewer than 20 pure observations dropped. As in Figure~\ref {fig:tair}, the shaded \ensuremath  {0\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}--\ensuremath  {4\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}} region is the across-site range of published rain--snow air-temperature thresholds \citep  {jennings2018spatial}, not the scored near-freezing subset. Because CSI excludes correct negatives, it scores each phase on its own terms and so exposes what accuracy hides in a snow-dominated sample. Every threshold method collapses toward zero CSI on the far side of its own cutoff, most visibly for the rain event class below \ensuremath  {0\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}, where a threshold that calls everything snow earns no credit at all. MRoS-XGB retains skill on both event classes across the full range. The values are not comparable between bins or between domains, since CSI depends on how often the event occurs (Table~\ref {tab:metrics}).}}{15}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:csi}{{7}{15}{}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {d}Transferable methods}{15}{}\protected@file@percent }
\citation{jennings2019sensitivity}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The fitted uncertainty envelope and where observer-reported mixed events fall relative to it, for California (left) and Colorado (right). Solid and dashed curves are the snow and rain thresholds of Equation~\ref {eq:band}: the shaded interior is the envelope, $0.30/0.70$ at both temperature extremes and opening to $0.15/0.85$ at $T_w = \ensuremath  {0\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}$. Each marker is one mixed event, filled where the envelope captured it and crossed where it did not. Mix was withheld from training entirely, so every marker is an out-of-sample test. Note how misses are overwhelmingly close to $p(\mathrm  {snow}) \approx 0$ or $\approx 1$, so the model was confident when wrong rather than marginally short of the threshold. Widening the band would therefore recover few of them while sacrificing confident rain--snow coverage. Figure~\ref {fig:mix-capture} resolves the same events by wet-bulb bin.}}{16}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:band}{{8}{16}{}{figure.8}{}}
