\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A1}{\ignorespaces Kriged predictor surfaces on the \mbox  {1-km} analysis grid for (a, top) California at 2024-02-04 17:00 UTC and (b, bottom) Colorado at 2024-02-03 17:00 UTC, two independent storm timestamps. Within each block, panels (a)--(c) are the kriged MRoS phase probabilities and panels (d)--(g) are kriged air, dewpoint and wet-bulb temperature and relative humidity. Open circles mark contributing stations, red triangles MRoS reports at that timestamp. The fields are smooth by construction, representing mesoscale structure rather than local microclimate, and predictor quality degrades away from the observation clusters, most visibly in the sparsely instrumented southern Sierra. Note that these are the full-data surfaces; the model predictor is the noisier leave-one-out version, which excludes the training report at its own location.}}{18}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:interpolations}{{A1}{18}{}{figure.1}{}}
\bibcite{jennings2025}{{18}{2025}{{Jennings et~al.}}{{}}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A2}{\ignorespaces Held-out test confusion matrices for California (left, $n = 2{,}301$) and Colorado (right, $n = 524$), scored at the raw $0.5$ threshold with the uncertainty envelope not applied and observer-reported mix excluded. Cells give counts and, in parentheses, the row-normalized share of each observed class, so the diagonal entries are snow and rain recall. Panel titles report accuracy and, taking snow as the event class, the probability of detection (POD) and false alarm ratio (FAR). The two domains reach nearly the same accuracy by different routes. Colorado's snow FAR is half California's (3.2\% against 6.2\%) because its rain observations are few and rarely called snow, but the same scarcity leaves its rain recall lower (86.5\% against 89.3\%) on a base of only 96 rain observations, which is the imbalance the macro F1 and CSI comparisons of Section~\ref {sec:bench-results} are meant to expose.}}{19}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:confusion}{{A2}{19}{}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A3}{\ignorespaces Reliability diagrams and calibrated score distributions for California (left pair) and Colorado (right pair), validation and test. Beta calibration (colored) moves the raw XGBoost curve (gray) toward the diagonal; the shaded region marks the maximum extent of the uncertainty band at $T_w = \ensuremath  {0\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}$ (0.15--0.85). The bimodal score histograms show that most predictions are confident, with the band occupying the sparse middle. Only pure snow and rain observations enter these diagrams, since calibration is defined against a binary outcome; mix is assessed separately in Figure~\ref {fig:band}. Brier scores in the panel titles use the same subset, raw versus calibrated. Departures from the diagonal persist in the outermost bins after calibration, for two reasons. Beta calibration is a three-parameter monotone map, so it corrects the overall shape of the reliability curve but cannot bend to fit a deviation confined to the extremes. And because the score distribution is bimodal, a quantile-spaced bin at either end spans a very narrow range of predicted probability while collecting a large share of the observations, so its mean prediction is pinned near 0 or 1 while a small number of confidently misclassified observations moves its observed frequency vertically. The residual therefore reflects cases where the model was confident and wrong, which no calibrator can remove, rather than a systematic bias in the map itself.}}{19}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:calibration}{{A3}{19}{}{figure.3}{}}
