\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A4}{\ignorespaces Model performance in \ensuremath  {1\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}} air-temperature bins for California (top) and Colorado (bottom), validation (dashed) and test (solid), with shading marking $\pm \ensuremath  {1\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}$ about freezing. Panels show snow F1, rain F1, the flag rate, and the mix-capture rate. In this air-temperature counterpart to Figure~\ref {fig:twet}, snow F1 declines as temperature rises, rain F1 improves, and the flag rate peaks just above \ensuremath  {0\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}, confirming that the envelope concentrates where phase is ambiguous under either temperature variable. Colorado's curves are noisier throughout, reflecting its smaller sample. Conventions follow Figure~\ref {fig:twet}, except that bins span \ensuremath  {-8\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}} to \ensuremath  {8\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}} because air and wet-bulb temperature occupy different ranges. A plotted zero, as in Colorado's test rain F1 near \ensuremath  {-0.5\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}, means both phases were present and the model recovered no rain.}}{20}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:f1-temp}{{A4}{20}{}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A5}{\ignorespaces Mean absolute SHAP value per predictor within \ensuremath  {1\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}} wet-bulb bins, over all observations, for California (top) and Colorado (bottom). California's reliance on elevation peaks sharply in the transition zone (0.151 at $T_w$ = 0 to \ensuremath  {1\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}), while Colorado's reliance on IMERG pLP climbs from 0.088 in the coldest bin to 0.203 in the warmest. As a ratio of mean $|$SHAP$|$ near freezing to away from freezing, usage rises for air temperature ($1.44\times $), elevation ($1.38\times $) and the MRoS mix surface ($1.14\times $), and falls for dewpoint ($0.84\times $). Bin populations are unequal, so a bright cell in an outer bin rests on far fewer observations than one near freezing, and empty bins are omitted rather than shown as zero.}}{21}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:shap-wetbulb}{{A5}{21}{}{figure.5}{}}
\bibcite{wayand2016}{{32}{2016}{{Wayand et~al.}}{{Wayand, Stimberis, Zagrodnik, Mass,, and Lundquist}}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A6}{\ignorespaces Ablation differences from the full configuration with 95\% cluster-bootstrap confidence intervals, for California (top) and Colorado (bottom): AUROC, overall accuracy, and near-freezing accuracy, each as baseline minus configuration, so positive values mean the removed predictors were contributing. Intervals are 2.5th--97.5th percentiles of 5{,}000 resamples over \mbox  {30-km} $\times $ calendar-day clusters, paired within resample. Markers are blue with a dark edge where the interval lies clear of zero and grey where it crosses zero, and configurations are ordered by the difference in the leftmost panel so vertical position is comparable across panels. The thermodynamics-only configuration separates cleanly from the full one on every metric in both domains, whereas most single-predictor removals stay within noise. Near-freezing accuracy carries wider intervals throughout, since it is computed on the $|T_w| \le \ensuremath  {2\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}$ subset alone.}}{22}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ablation-ci}{{A6}{22}{}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A7}{\ignorespaces Air-temperature counterpart to Figure~\ref {fig:mros-contrib}: near-freezing precision and recall for the full configuration versus the configuration without the MRoS LOOCV surfaces, in \ensuremath  {1\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}} air-temperature bins on the held-out test split, scored at the raw $0.5$ threshold with mix excluded. In California the loss now appears in rain recall rather than snow recall, because air temperature discriminates phase less sharply than wet-bulb temperature and its bins therefore combine thermodynamic regimes that the wet-bulb view separates. That the loss survives the change of binning variable while moving between phases indicates that the crowdsourced surfaces supply information the temperature fields do not carry. Colorado's rain curves are undefined or near-degenerate in several cold bins where its rain sample is only a handful of observations.}}{23}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mros-contrib-tair}{{A7}{23}{}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A8}{\ignorespaces Held-out accuracy of the model and the nine benchmark partitioning methods for California (top) and Colorado (bottom), overall (left) and on the near-freezing subset (right; $|T_w| \le \ensuremath  {2\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}$). Methods are ordered by overall accuracy within each domain and shaded by predictor family, with air-temperature thresholds in orange, dewpoint in green, wet-bulb in blue, and the two logistic methods in purple. This is the visual counterpart to Table~\ref {tab:benchmark}, which gives the same values with confidence intervals; the paired differences that support the significance claims appear in Figure~\ref {fig:forest}. Note that the near-freezing panel compresses the benchmark ordering, since every method loses skill there while the model loses less.}}{24}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accuracy-bars}{{A8}{24}{}{figure.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A9}{\ignorespaces Accuracy and per-phase bias against air temperature for all nine benchmark partitioning methods and the model, California (left) and Colorado (right), held-out test split, \ensuremath  {1\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}} bins with $n \ge 20$ pure observations. Panels give, from top to bottom, accuracy, snow bias, and rain bias, where bias is the signed difference between predicted and observed frequency of that phase within the bin, so zero is unbiased and positive means over-prediction. These are point estimates without intervals; Figure~\ref {fig:tair} gives the bootstrap-resampled version for the model and best benchmark. Bins holding fewer than 20 pure observations are dropped, so the curves do not all span the same range.}}{25}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tair-bias}{{A9}{25}{}{figure.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A10}{\ignorespaces Mix-capture rate by \ensuremath  {1\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}} wet-bulb bin for California (top) and Colorado (bottom), held-out test split. Points give the fraction of observer-reported mix events per bin whose calibrated $p(\mathrm  {snow})$ falls inside the uncertainty band, with 95\% intervals; grey bars give the mix-event count per bin on the right-hand axis, and shading marks $|T_w| \le \ensuremath  {1\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}$. Capture is highest in the near-freezing bins where mixed precipitation actually occurs ($47\%$ at $T_w = \ensuremath  {0.5\,^{\circ }\mathrm  {C}}$ in California) and falls away on either side, the intended behavior for a band placed to flag ambiguity rather than to maximize capture everywhere. Pooled capture, $31\%$ in California and $36\%$ in Colorado, agrees with the independent test-set bootstrap estimates of $0.30$ and $0.33$ in Table~\ref {tab:bootstrap}.}}{26}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mix-capture}{{A10}{26}{}{figure.10}{}}
